    <section class="essay-sources" id="further-reading">
      <h2>Sources and further reading</h2>
      <p>Expanded and checked 15 October 2026. The shop is an invented teaching example. PostgreSQL 18.6 server experiments reproduced both price-view schedules, Repeatable Read write skew, a Serializable rejection and complete retry, and the control-row comparison at Read Committed and Repeatable Read. A third observation connection confirmed the control-lock wait; the two staff connections performed the decisions. The sandbox uses PostgreSQL 18.3 through PGlite 0.5.8 and demonstrates single-connection transaction state, not these overlaps.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-iso.html">PostgreSQL 18: Transaction isolation</a> — ordinary views, conflicting writes, and the distinction between Repeatable Read and Serializable.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-set-transaction.html">SET TRANSACTION</a> — selecting the level, read-only mode, and when a transaction’s characteristics can change.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/explicit-locking.html">Explicit locking</a> — a next reading when the rule calls for deliberate coordination of records.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html">Serialization failure handling</a> — why the failed transaction’s reads and decisions must be retried as well as its writes.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
